@* What a SAS URL needs to allow, shown wherever one is asked for. *@
<div class="alert alert-info small mb-3 se-az-note" role="note">
    <div class="d-flex align-items-start gap-2">
        <span class="oi oi-key mt-1" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <div>
            <strong>Give the SAS these permissions: Read, Create, Write and List.</strong>
            Leave Delete off: the editor never deletes anything.
            @if (Full)
            {
                <ul class="mb-2 mt-1 ps-3">
                    @foreach (var (letter, name, use) in AzureSas.Needed)
                    {
                        <li><strong>@name</strong> (@letter): @use.</li>
                    }
                </ul>
                <div class="mb-1">
                    Make it for the <strong>container</strong> (not a single file), so you can browse, and so the old file can be kept in a .OLD folder.
                    In the Azure portal: Storage account › Containers › your container › <strong>Shared access tokens</strong>, tick the four permissions,
                    set an expiry date, Allowed protocols <strong>HTTPS only</strong>, then <strong>Generate SAS token and URL</strong> and copy the
                    <strong>Blob SAS URL</strong>. In Azure Storage Explorer: right-click the container › <strong>Get Shared Access Signature</strong>.
                </div>
                <div class="mb-0">
                    A SAS URL works for anyone who has it until it expires, so pick the shortest expiry that suits you.
                    Read and List are enough if you only open courses here; saving needs Create and Write.
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter] public bool Full { get; set; }
}
